From: prospero-win orbis <noreply@localhost>
Subject: server: orbis: keep a blocking reply descriptor when O_NONBLOCK is refused

Measured on the PS4 (console run 12): fcntl(F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK) on the
reply descriptor a client passed with SCM_RIGHTS fails with EACCES when
the descriptor is a socket pair end, and FIONBIO is refused as well
(run 13). init_thread then gave up without a reply, which the client
waits for forever. Wine's pipes are kernel pipes on Orbis now
(wine/orbis/pw_wine_compat_libc.c), where O_NONBLOCK works and this path
is not taken; with WINE_ORBIS_KERNEL_PIPE=0 they are socket pairs again,
and the thread then keeps a blocking reply descriptor (64-byte replies
to a client that reads them at once) instead of dying.

diff --git a/server/thread.c b/server/thread.c
index a6fe880b11..1607fc9d8e 100644
--- a/server/thread.c
+++ b/server/thread.c
@@ -1874,7 +1874,20 @@ static int init_thread( struct thread *thread, int reply_fd, int wait_fd )
         goto error;
     }
 
-    if (fcntl( reply_fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK ) == -1) goto error;
+    if (fcntl( reply_fd, F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK ) == -1)
+    {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+        /* Measured on the PS4 (run 12): EACCES on a passed socket pair end,
+         * and FIONBIO is refused as well. Wine's pipes are kernel pipes on
+         * Orbis, where O_NONBLOCK works; with WINE_ORBIS_KERNEL_PIPE=0 they
+         * are socket pairs again, and a blocking reply descriptor still
+         * carries the 64-byte replies to a client that reads them at once. */
+        fprintf( stderr, "wineserver: orbis: init_thread %04x: O_NONBLOCK on reply fd %d refused (errno %d), going on blocking\n",
+                 thread->id, reply_fd, errno );
+#else
+        goto error;
+#endif
+    }
 
     thread->reply_fd = create_anonymous_fd( &thread_fd_ops, reply_fd, &thread->obj, 0 );
     thread->wait_fd  = create_anonymous_fd( &thread_fd_ops, wait_fd, &thread->obj, 0 );
